@page "/control-your-internet"

<PageTitle>Take Back Your Internet - DoYouOwnIt</PageTitle>
<div class="site-grid">
	<div class="left-column"></div>
 <div class="main-content-column">
		<nav aria-label="breadcrumb">
			<ol class="breadcrumb">
				<li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/">Home</a></li>
				<li class="breadcrumb-item active">Take Back Your Internet</li>
			</ol>
		</nav>


		<h1>Take Back Your Internet</h1>
		<hr />
		<article class="why-this-page">
			<h2>Why am I adding this page?</h2>
			<p>
				I’m adding this page because I’m tired of the current internet landscape of slop content being forced down your throat, 
				having the stuff you want to pay attention to be hard to find, and apps that constantly annoy you to get your attention.
			</p>
			<p>
				Back before TikTok became popular, people could control their feeds and get the information that they were looking with no constant distractions. 
				Now constant recommendation algorithms flood everything and it has become difficult to wade through an endless ocean of garbage to get to the stuff you care about.
			</p>
			<p>
				Here are some tools to get back the control of your internet experience:
			</p>

		</article>


		<article class="services">
			<div class="service-section">
				<h2>Information About Companies and Services Trying to Take Advantage of You</h2>
				<article id="consumer-rights-wiki">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="https://images.gmntgstrg.com/defaultandresourceimages/consumer_rights_wiki_logo.png" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(200px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="Consumer Rights Wiki Logo Containing a Globe With An Open Book and a Lock at the bottom of the Globe" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://consumerrights.wiki/w/Main_Page"><h3>Consumer Rights Wiki</h3></a>
						<p>
							Created by Louis Rossman, the Consumer Rights Wiki is a wiki that contains information about all of the different ways companies are trying to 
							take advantage of their clients with awful practices. They also have browser extension that lets users of it know of bad practices that a companies
							are currently doing with their products (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/consumer-rights-wiki/bppajinomefndbbmopljhbdfefnefdha?pli=1">Chrome</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/consumer-rights-wiki/">Firefox</a>).
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
				<h2>Control Your Own Feed</h2>
				<article id="feedbro">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="images/outside-sources/Feedbro Icon.png" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(200px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="Feedbro with a Computer Computer Monitor and an RSS Feed icon" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://nodetics.com/feedbro/"><h3>Feedbro</h3></a>
						<p>
							Feedbro is an RSS feed reader extension for your web browser that allows you to not only use RSS feeds that different websites provide
							but also gives you the option to make feeds based on social media accounts.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
				<article id="control-panel-for-twitter">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="https://soitis.dev/images/control-panel-for-twitter.svg" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(200px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="Crane with white feathers and black beak and feet" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://soitis.dev/control-panel-for-twitter"><h3>Control Panel For Twitter (X)</h3></a>
						<p>
							In case you're still browsing the hell known as Twitter (aka X), then this is a must to have.
							It get's rid of the <strong>For You</strong> tab and instead separates the posts from shared posts in two separate tabs.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
				<article id="control-panel-for-twitter">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="https://nitter.net/logo.png" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(200px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="Crane with white feathers and black beak and feet" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://twiiit.com/"><h3>Twiiit</h3></a>
						<p>
							If someone has a Twitter(X) account that you want to check out but don't want a twitter account Twiiit can redirect you do different
							public instances of a service called <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://nitter.net/">Nitter</a>. Nitter basically allows you to check different Twitter(X)
							feeds without having to sign up for the website. There is also instances such as <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://xcancel.com/">xcancel</a>.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
			<div class="service-section">
				<h2>Create Your Own Streaming Service</h2>
				<article id="jellyfin">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="images/outside-sources/Jellyfin Icon.png" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(200px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="Jellyfin logo" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://jellyfin.org/"><h3>Jellyfin</h3></a>
						<p>
							Create your own custom media hosting server to host different Movies, TV Shows, Music, Books, Photos, Live TV and more.
							It includes SyncPlay, which allows you to share your media remotely.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
				<article id="plex">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="images/outside-sources/Plex_logo_2022.svg" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(150px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="Plex Logo" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.plex.tv/your-media/"><h3>Plex</h3></a>
						<p>
							Plex also allows you to create your own media servers for movies and television shows.
							Additionally, it allows you to sync-up other streaming services for you to choose from.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
			<div class="service-section">
				<h2>Control Your Interfaces</h2>
				<article id="stylus">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="images/outside-sources/stylus128.png" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(200px, 100%); max-height: 300px;"
							 alt="S between brackets" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://add0n.com/stylus.html/"><h3>Stylus</h3></a>
						<p>
							A browser extension that allows you to customize different website interfaces by editing different CSS elements.
							You can create your own, or use different user generated styles to customize the look and feel of different websites you might use on a daily basis.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
			<div class="live-stream-section">
				<h2>Create Your Own Live Stream Site</h2>
				<article id="owncast">
					<div class="service-image-column">
						<img src="https://images.gmntgstrg.com/defaultandresourceimages/owncast.svg" class="img-thumbnail"
							 style="max-width: min(100px, 100%); max-height: 200px;"
							 alt="S between brackets" />
					</div>
					<div class="service-explanation">
						<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://owncast.online/"><h3>Owncast</h3></a>
						<p>
							While it requires a bit more technical know-how, Owncast is a open source software that allows you to host your own personal live streaming
							service with it's own custom chat features, customizable interface, and has software documentation to help you get started and personalize
							your own streaming site.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
		</article>
    </div>
	<div class="right-column"></div>
</div>
